.TH RC.SUBR 1M "30 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
rc.subr \- shell functions for the boot scripts of the add-on packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft CW
\&. /usr/lib/amix/rc.subr
rc_enabled \fIservice\fP [ yes | no ] || exit 0
.ft R
.fi
.PP
.B rc_log
.I service message ...
.br
.B rc_enabled
.I service
[
.B yes
|
.B no
]
.br
.B rc_conf
.I service key
[
.I default
]
.br
.B rc_bg
.I seconds service command
[
.I argument ...
]
.br
.B rc_mknod
.I node
.B c
|
.B b
.IR major | \-
.I minor
[
.I module
[
.I mode
] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B /usr/lib/amix/rc.subr
is read by the boot script of every service an add-on package installs in
.BR /etc/init.d .
It gives each script the same way to be turned on and off, the same log and
the same way to run something slow without holding up the boot.
It is written for the system's own
.BR /bin/sh .
.TP
.B rc_log
appends one line to the boot log: the date and time, the
.IR service ,
and the
.IR message .
.TP
.B rc_enabled
succeeds when
.I service
is on: when
.BR /etc/rc.conf 's
line
.IB service =
says
.BR yes ,
or, with no such line, when the default given here is
.B yes
(without one, the default is
.BR no ).
A script names its own default, so the package decides whether a new
service starts until the administrator says otherwise.
.TP
.B rc_conf
prints the value of the line
.IB service _ key =
in
.BR /etc/rc.conf ,
or
.I default
when there is none.
.TP
.B rc_bg
runs
.I command
in the background and returns at once.
The command's output is appended to
.BI /var/adm/rclog/ service\fR;
the boot log gets one line when it starts and one with its exit status
when it ends.
If it is still running after
.I seconds
it is sent a terminate signal, and five seconds later a kill signal if it
is still there; the log says so.
Only the command itself is stopped, not programs it started, so a script
given to
.B rc_bg
should bound what it runs.
.TP
.B rc_mknod
makes the device
.I node
of type
.B c
or
.BR b ,
with the given numbers and
.I mode
(default 0666), unless it already exists as exactly that device.
When
.I module
is given and
.B kbootset major
names that module's major number in the running kernel's registry, that
number is used; if it differs from
.IR major ,
the difference is reported on the console and in the log as a packaging
error.
Otherwise
.I major
is used as given;
.B \-
says there is none, and then a module the registry does not know gets no
node, which the log says.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /usr/lib/amix/rc.subr
this library.
.TP
.B /etc/rc.conf
the services' switches and settings.
.TP
.B /var/adm/rc.log
the boot log.
.TP
.BI /var/adm/rclog/ service
the output of a command run with
.BR rc_bg .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rc.conf (4),
.BR kbootset (1M)
.SH NOTES
The helpers need
.B /usr
mounted, so they serve scripts that start after the file systems are
mounted (from
.B S20
on in
.BR /etc/rc2.d ).
Every variable they set begins with
.BR _rc ,
so a script's own variables are left alone.
